Q: C++ custom allocator as pool of objects A: <pre><code>auto iter = _freeList.rbegin();
T* out = *iter;
_freeList.erase( (iter++).base() );
</code></pre>
<p>This exhibits undefined behavior. <code>(iter++).base()</code> is the same as <code>iter.base()</code> (as you are using a post-increment), which in turn is the same as <code>_freeList.end()</code>.</p>
<p>My guess is, this <code>erase()</code> call has no effect in practice, and the size of <code>_freeList</code> is not reduced. Then in a matching <code>deallocate</code> call the size is increased, and goes over <code>K_Size</code>.</p>
<hr />
<p>This is also unnecessarily complicated. Something like this should be clearer and easier on the eyes:</p>
<pre><code>T* out = _freeList.back();
_freeList.pop_back();
</code></pre>

Q: How to list Zebra device profiles using .NET MAUI A: <p>According to the official document about <a href="https://zebra-techdocs.github.io/datawedge/latest/guide/api/getprofileslist/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Get Profiles List</a>, the intent should be:</p>
<pre><code>    global::Android.Content.Intent dwIntent = new global::Android.Content.Intent();
    dwIntent.SetAction(&quot;com.symbol.datawedge.api.ACTION&quot;);
    dwIntent.putExtra(&quot;com.symbol.datawedge.api.GET_PROFILES_LIST&quot;, &quot;&quot;);
    _context.SendBroadcast(dwIntent);
</code></pre>
<p>And receive the list in the BroadcastReceiver:</p>
<pre><code>    [BroadcastReceiver(Exported = true)]
    [IntentFilter(new[] {&quot;com.symbol.datawedge.api.ACTION&quot;})]
    public class ListReceiver : BroadcastReceiver
    {
        public override void OnReceive(Context? context, Intent? intent)
        {
            //var bundler = intent.Extras;
            if (intent.HasExtra(&quot;com.symbol.datawedge.api.RESULT_GET_PROFILES_LIST&quot;))
            {
                var list = intent.GetStringArrayExtra(&quot;com.symbol.datawedge.api.RESULT_GET_PROFILES_LIST&quot;);
            }
        }
    }
</code></pre>

Q: Objects.equals(null, null); should return false A: <p>In principle, your implementation of <strong>equals()</strong> is not the best. On the other hand, it depends on the needs of your program. If you need (I prefer to avoid working with null objects) the comparison of two null objects to return false (in reality, only the object passed as a parameter can be null), make the appropriate implementation without depending on that of <em>Object</em>.</p>
<pre><code>  // must receive an “Object” to override the ‘equals’ of “Object” 
@Override
public boolean equals( Object other ) {

   // I would never pass a null parameter, but it can happen...
   if( other == null ) {
      return false;
   }
   if( other instanceof Person aux ) {
      // since nothing prevents two people from being named “John Waine,”
      // being 87 years old, and having a rooster named “Donald,” we use the “id,” and
      // no, you should never have a *Person* object with a null “id”
      return id == aux.id;
   }
   return false;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Copy font to Zebra printer from cmd A: <p>If you have node-js installed, you can use the following bit of code.
Save as file sendfont.js, then run as:</p>
<pre><code>&gt; node sendfont.js c:\path\to\font.ttf R:MYFONT.FON printer-addr
</code></pre>
<p>The code sends the font using <code>~DU</code>, then queries the printer to ensure the font installed correctly.  You may want to add smarter error checking on that query.  It currently just displays the result of the <code>^HW</code> command.</p>
<pre><code>// sendfont.js
const fs = require('fs');
const net = require('net');

if (process.argv.length != 5) {
    console.log('Usage: node sendfont.js font-path zplname printer-addr');
    process.exit(1);
}

const path = process.argv[2];   // e.g. c:\\path\\to\\font.ttf
const zname = process.argv[3];  // e.g. R:MYFONT.FON
const addr = process.argv[4];   // hostname or ip address

let buf = fs.readFileSync(path);
let zpl = '~DU' + zname + ',' + buf.length + ',' +
          buf.toString('hex');

// Send the font, then query to verify
(async () =&gt; {
    await zsend(addr, zpl);
    console.log('' + (await zsend(addr, '^XA^HW' + zname + '^XZ', true)));
    process.exit(0);
})();

function zsend(addr, text, recv) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {
        let timer = null;
        let result = null;
        const client = new net.Socket();

        client.setNoDelay();
        client.connect(9100, addr, () =&gt; {
            client.write(text, () =&gt; {
                if (recv) {
                    timer = setTimeout(resolve, 2500);
                } else {
                    resolve();
                }
            });
        });

        client.on('data', (data) =&gt; {
            result = result ? Buffer.concat([result, data]) : data;
            if (timer) {
                clearTimeout(timer);
            }
            // Wait for more data
            timer = setTimeout(()=&gt;{ resolve(result); }, 200);
        });
        client.on('error', (err) =&gt; {
            reject(err);
        });
    });
}
</code></pre>
<p>I pulled that code together from existing code.  Hopefully it works.  Don't have a printer to test with where I am currently.</p>
